## Mathematical Convention

For a relation R, R^{-1}={(v,u):(u,v) in R}. Domain is the set of actual first components, range the set of actual second components; duplicates do not add members.
Swapping x and y reflects the graph in y=x. A function has exactly one output for each member of its domain.
"Equal to its own inverse" means R=R^{-1}; it does not mean that R is a reflexive relation.
All algebra variables are real. A semicircular arc and a line segment include their endpoints unless the question excludes them.

## U01 / 425a

R arrows: 1 -> 20, 2 -> 30, 3 -> 40, 4 -> 50, 5 -> 50.
Inverse arrows: 20 -> 1, 30 -> 2, 40 -> 3, 50 -> 4, 50 -> 5.
D(R)={1,2,3,4,5}; V(R)={20,30,40,50}.
D(R^{-1})={20,30,40,50}; V(R^{-1})={1,2,3,4,5}.
Draw the two member sets and precisely these arrows. The repeated output 50 is one member with two inverse arrows, not two copies of 50.

## U02 / 425b

R arrows: 1/2 -> 3, 1/3 -> 4, 2/3 -> 5, 1/4 -> 5, 3/4 -> 7.
Inverse arrows: 3 -> 1/2, 4 -> 1/3, 5 -> 2/3, 5 -> 1/4, 7 -> 3/4.
D(R)={1/2,1/3,2/3,1/4,3/4}; V(R)={3,4,5,7}.
D(R^{-1})={3,4,5,7}; V(R^{-1})={1/2,1/3,2/3,1/4,3/4}.
The repeated output 5 gives two inverse arrows; ordering of set members is immaterial.

## U03 / 426a

R^{-1}={(7,sqrt(2)),(8,sqrt(3)),(10,sqrt(5)),(11,sqrt(6)),(12,sqrt(7))}.
D(R)={sqrt(2),sqrt(3),sqrt(5),sqrt(6),sqrt(7)}; V(R)={7,8,10,11,12}.
D(R^{-1})={7,8,10,11,12}; V(R^{-1})={sqrt(2),sqrt(3),sqrt(5),sqrt(6),sqrt(7)}.
Each given pair has been individually reversed; no irrational member is approximated.

## U04 / 426b

R^{-1}={(1,0.3),(0,sqrt(10)),(1,-2),(0,sqrt(6)),(1,13)}.
D(R)={0.3,sqrt(10),-2,sqrt(6),13}; V(R)={0,1}.
D(R^{-1})={0,1}; V(R^{-1})={0.3,sqrt(10),-2,sqrt(6),13}.
There are five inverse pairs but only two inverse domain members.

## U05 / 426c

R^{-1}={(12,1),(6,2),(4,3),(3,4),(2,6),(1,12)}=R.
D(R)=V(R)=D(R^{-1})=V(R^{-1})={1,2,3,4,6,12}.
The pairs (1,12)/(12,1), (2,6)/(6,2), (3,4)/(4,3) are all present in both orientations.

## U06 / 427a

Inverse predicate for the same ordered argument names: American city A lies south of American city B.
If (A,B) satisfies north-of, then (B,A) satisfies south-of. No city names need to be invented.

## U07 / 427b

Inverse: the first person is younger than the second. Reversal changes older-than to younger-than, not to same-age or not-older-than.

## U08 / 427c

Original x=y+2. Inverse y=x+2, equivalently x=y-2: the first number is 2 less than the second.

## U09 / 427d

Original x=y/5 with x,y>0. Inverse y=x/5, equivalently x=5y with x,y>0.
The first positive number is five times the second positive number; positivity remains.

## U10 / 428

From the two already-viewed images:
R={(-2,1),(-1,-1),(1,-2),(3,-1),(4,1)}.
R^{-1}={(1,-2),(-1,-1),(-2,1),(-1,3),(1,4)}.
In coordinate axes, plot exactly those five isolated points for each relation. Do not connect them into lines or curves. The point (-1,-1) is fixed by reflection; other shared points are permitted.
D(R)={-2,-1,1,3,4}; V(R)={-2,-1,1}; inverse swaps these sets.
R is a function: each of its five distinct first coordinates has exactly one second coordinate.
R^{-1} is not a function: input -1 has outputs -1 and 3, and input 1 has outputs -2 and 4. Input -2 has only output 1; this does not rescue the failed uniqueness elsewhere.

## U11 / 429

The three guaranteed inverse graph points are (1,-8), (7,1), (9,0).
Any two distinct points from this list suffice, for example (1,-8) and (7,1).
The question does not say these are the only points in the relation, so the answer should not assert that its complete graph consists of only these three points.

## U12 / 430

Plot filled endpoints A=(-3,2), B=(4,5) and join them as a segment.
Reflect endpoints to A'=(2,-3), B'=(5,4); join these filled endpoints as the inverse segment in the same coordinate system.
Slope of AB is (5-2)/(4-(-3))=3/7; f has y=(3x+23)/7 for -3<=x<=4.
Inverse segment has y=(7x-23)/3 for 2<=x<=5.
D(f)=[-3,4]; V(f)=[2,5]; D(f^{-1})=[2,5]; V(f^{-1})=[-3,4].
All four intervals are closed: the segment contains both endpoints. The inverse segment is the entire reflection, not an extrapolated infinite line.

## U13 / 431

Original upper semicircular arc: x^2+y^2=9, y>=0; y=sqrt(9-x^2), -3<=x<=3.
Reflect in y=x to obtain the right semicircular arc x^2+y^2=9, x>=0.
Inverse consists of both branches y=+sqrt(9-x^2) and y=-sqrt(9-x^2), 0<=x<=3.
Its endpoints are (0,-3) and (0,3), both included; its rightmost point is (3,0).
Draw the complete right arc, not just the first-quadrant arc or a full circle.
Inverse domain [0,3], range [-3,3]. It is not a function: at x=0 the two outputs are -3 and 3, and at x=1 they are -sqrt(8) and sqrt(8). At x=3 the branches coincide at 0 only.

## U14 / 432a

Inverse equation: y-5x=3. Not self-inverse.
(3,0) belongs to x-5y=3, whereas the reversed point (0,3) does not (-15!=3).

## U15 / 432b

Inverse equation: y+3x=1. Not self-inverse.
(1,0) belongs to x+3y=1, but (0,1) gives 3!=1.

## U16 / 432c

Inverse equation: y+x=5, equivalently x+y=5.
Self-inverse: addition is commutative, so every solution remains a solution after swapping coordinates.

## U17 / 432d

Inverse equation: y^2-x=0, equivalently x=y^2.
Not self-inverse: (2,4) satisfies x^2-y=0; (4,2) does not (16-2!=0).
Do not discard the negative branch when expressing the inverse as y=+/-sqrt(x), x>=0.

## U18 / 432e

Inverse equation: 2y^2+5y=x-x^2, equivalently x^2-x+2y^2+5y=0.
Not self-inverse: (0,1) satisfies 2x^2+5x=y-y^2, but (1,0) does not (7!=0).
Swapping must move both quadratic and linear terms, not just the variable on one side.

## U19 / 432f

Inverse equation: yx=10, equivalently xy=10.
Self-inverse: multiplication is commutative. Both coordinates are nonzero in every solution.

## U20 / 432g

Inverse equation: y^2+x^2=9, equivalently x^2+y^2=9.
Self-inverse: the two squares enter symmetrically. This is the whole radius-3 circle, unlike the restricted semicircle of 431.

## U21 / 432h

Inverse equation: y^2-x^2=12, equivalently x^2-y^2=-12.
Not self-inverse: (sqrt(12),0) belongs to the original, while (0,sqrt(12)) gives -12, not 12.
The right-hand side must change sign if the inverse is reordered back to x^2-y^2.

## U22 / 432i

Inverse equation: y^2-x^2=0, equivalently x^2-y^2=0.
Self-inverse: negating a zero equation preserves its solution set, the union y=x and y=-x.
The complete self-inverse selection for 432 is exactly {c,f,g,i}.
The counterexamples in a,b,d,e,h prove that no further item belongs to this selection.

## U23 / 433a

h: x-2y=4, or y=x/2-2. Draw the infinite line through (0,-2) and (4,0).
p: y-2x=4, or y=2x+4. Draw the infinite line through (-2,0) and (0,4).
These pairs of points are coordinate swaps, establishing reflection in y=x. There are no segment/domain restrictions in this exercise.

## U24 / 433b

h: x/2-y/3=1, or y=3x/2-3. Draw the infinite line through (0,-3) and (2,0).
p: y/2-x/3=1, or y=2x/3+2. Draw the infinite line through (-3,0) and (0,2).
Swapping coordinates carries the two h intercepts to the two p intercepts.

## U25 / 433c

h: y=x^2, an upward-opening parabola with vertex (0,0), axis x=0, passing through (-2,4),(-1,1),(1,1),(2,4).
p: x=y^2, a right-opening parabola with vertex (0,0), axis y=0, passing through (4,-2),(1,-1),(1,1),(4,2).
Draw smooth complete parabolas through these points. The p graph includes both y=+sqrt(x) and y=-sqrt(x), x>=0, rather than a single function branch.

## U26 / 433d

h: y=-(x-2)^2, a downward-opening parabola, vertex (2,0), axis x=2.
Useful h points: (1,-1),(3,-1),(0,-4),(4,-4).
p: (y-2)^2+x=0, equivalently x=-(y-2)^2, a left-opening parabola, vertex (0,2), axis y=2.
Corresponding p points: (-1,1),(-1,3),(-4,0),(-4,4).
Draw both complete reflected parabolas. Equivalently p has y=2+/-sqrt(-x) for x<=0; both branches are required.

## U27 / 434a

(b+4)^2-1=(b+3)(b+5).
b^3+125=(b+5)(b^2-5b+25).
Cancel b+5 on the original domain to obtain \frac{b+3}{b^2-5b+25}, with b!=-5 retained.
b^2-5b+25=(b-5/2)^2+75/4>0 for every real b, so -5 is the only real original denominator exclusion.
The canceled expression is otherwise defined at -5, which must not be silently added to the original domain.

## U28 / 434b

(p-5)^2-4=(p-7)(p-3).
27-p^3=(3-p)(p^2+3p+9)=-(p-3)(p^2+3p+9).
Cancel p-3 with its minus sign to obtain \frac{7-p}{p^2+3p+9}, with p!=3 retained.
p^2+3p+9=(p+3/2)^2+27/4>0; there are no other real exclusions.
The alternative -\frac{p-7}{p^2+3p+9} is equivalent. A positive (p-7) numerator would be wrong.

## U29 / 434c

(x-2)^3+27=((x-2)+3)((x-2)^2-3(x-2)+9)=(x+1)(x^2-7x+19).
x^3+1=(x+1)(x^2-x+1).
Cancel x+1 to obtain \frac{x^2-7x+19}{x^2-x+1}, with x!=-1 retained.
Expansion checks: (x+1)(x^2-7x+19)=x^3-6x^2+12x+19=(x-2)^3+27.
x^2-x+1=(x-1/2)^2+3/4>0. The two residual quadratics share no factor: their difference -6x+18 would require a shared root x=3, but the denominator at 3 is 7.
The modern formula is already approved; this factorization check is not a new source-text review.

## U30 / 434d

(a+1)^3+1=(a+2)((a+1)^2-(a+1)+1)=(a+2)(a^2+a+1).
a^3-1=(a-1)(a^2+a+1).
Cancel a^2+a+1 to obtain \frac{a+2}{a-1}, with a!=1.
a^2+a+1=(a+1/2)^2+3/4>0, so this canceled quadratic contributes no additional real exclusions.
Expansion checks: (a+2)(a^2+a+1)=a^3+3a^2+3a+2=(a+1)^3+1.

## U31 / 435a

8^{-3}*(1/2)^{-10}=(2^3)^{-3}*2^{10}=2^{-9+10}=2.
Negative exponent reciprocates the nonzero base; it does not negate the value.

## U32 / 435b

81^5*9^{-11}=(9^2)^5*9^{-11}=9^{10-11}=9^{-1}=\frac{1}{9}.

## U33 / 435c

1.5^3=(3/2)^3=27/8.
(3/8)^{-1}=8/3; (-1/3)^{-1}=-3.
Thus (27/8)*(8/3)+(-3)=9-3=6.
The sum is not inside the scope of either exponent.

